41 performance of Weber's Der Freischütz, which is referenced within the story."}, {"sourceSlug": "gothic-literature", "sourceName": "Gothic Literature", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "the-phantom-of-the-opera", "targetName": "The Phantom of the Opera", "context": "The Phantom of the Opera is a quintessential example of Gothic literature, embodying its themes of horror and romance."}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Setting"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Cultural Center"}, {"name": "London, England", "role": "Theater Hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: The Phantom of the Opera", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "the-phantom-of-the-opera"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q272506", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Phantom_of_the_Opera_(novel)"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "The Phantom of the Opera contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Phantom of the Opera reshaped the landscape of modern literature and theater, influencing the portrayal of complex characters and themes in subsequent works, while its adaptations have reached an audience of millions globally.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T13:05:20Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30095299486", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Phantom of the Opera is a Gothic horror novel by French author Gaston Leroux. It was first published as a serial in Le Gaulois from 23 September 1909 to 8 January 1910, and was released in volume form in late March 1910 by Pierre Lafitte. The novel is partly inspired by historical events at the Paris Opera during the nineteenth century, and by an apocryphal tale concerning the use of a former ballet pupil's skeleton in an 1841 production of Carl Maria von Weber's Der Freischütz. It has been successfully adapted into various stage and film adaptations, most notable of which are the 1925 film depiction featuring Lon Chaney, and Andrew Lloyd Webber's 1986 musical.", "newValue": "The Phantom of the Opera, a Gothic horror novel penned by French author Gaston Leroux, first appeared as a serial in the newspaper Le Gaulois from September 23, 1909, to January 8, 1910, before its volume release in March 1910 by Pierre Lafitte. Set against the backdrop of the Paris Opera, the narrative intricately weaves a tale of love, obsession, and the supernatural, reflecting the artistic and cultural milieu of early 20th-century France. This work matters significantly as it encapsulates the era's fascination with the macabre and the romantic, while also addressing themes of social class and isolation, resonating with a global audience across generations.

The novel follows the enigmatic figure known as the Phantom, who resides beneath the Paris Opera House, haunting its corridors and influencing the lives of its performers, notably Christine Daaé. His obsession with Christine leads to a series of dramatic events, including tragic confrontations and musical masterpieces that capture the essence of the operatic art form. Leroux's narrative is partly inspired by actual incidents at the Paris Opera, including the infamous 1841 performance of Carl Maria von Weber's Der Freischütz, where it is rumored that a deceased ballet pupil's skeleton was used, adding a layer of historical intrigue to the fiction.

